1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,720
Cent #8462.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:06,320
A penny struck in 1973, at Philadelphia.

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:08,400
Beside Lincoln's face,

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,131
someone has engraved a rabbit.

5
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:13,779
Under it, the word EASTER, just above the date.

6
00:00:14,539 --> 00:00:16,946
Collectors have nicknamed it the Easter penny.

7
00:00:17,859 --> 00:00:19,539
This is an exemplary instance

8
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,045
of the Vandalized trait.

9
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:23,800
The Traits Atlas of the CENTS Trait Council

10
00:00:23,879 --> 00:00:25,800
defines Vandalized as clear,

11
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,039
intentional, human-made deformation

12
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:29,443
or marking of the coin.

13
00:00:30,039 --> 00:00:31,960
This was one of the hardest traits to catalog

14
00:00:32,039 --> 00:00:34,137
due to the ambiguity of intentionality.

15
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:37,534
The Atlas gives this Cent as its first example.

16
00:00:38,539 --> 00:00:41,421
26 of the 10,000 Cents carry the trait.

17
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:44,954
Others carry scratches,

18
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:51,412
a drill attempt, paint, or a drawing in fine liner.

19
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:56,664
#8462 is the only one from 1973.

20
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,200
Compare Cent #2125,

21
00:01:00,359 --> 00:01:03,799
also from 1973, a Cent close to mint state.

22
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,893
The same design, the same year.

23
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:08,177
Only one of them was engraved.

24
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:10,665
Difference makes meaning.

25
00:01:11,439 --> 00:01:12,840
In the words of Steven Reiss,

26
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:15,719
in his article "Materiality and Signification":

27
00:01:15,852 --> 00:01:17,480
"Coins whose look strays farthest

28
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:19,719
from the norm became the most coveted.

29
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,560
Difference now drives value -

30
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:22,920
just as Saussure said:

31
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,507
we understand a sign by what it is not."

32
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:29,260
After it was photographed, this penny was melted,

33
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:30,700
with the rest of the 10,000,

34
00:01:30,819 --> 00:01:32,459
into a single copper block.

35
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:34,260
Its copper is in the block.

36
00:01:34,379 --> 00:01:36,646
Its image is inscribed on a satoshi.

37
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,400
The copper was altered twice:

38
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:42,537
once by an unknown engraver, once by melting.

39
00:01:43,659 --> 00:01:45,019
In the words of Martin Lehnen,

40
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:47,180
in his essay "Rutherford Chang's Conceptual

41
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:48,939
Collecting": "Can destruction

42
00:01:49,019 --> 00:01:51,659
create value, just as accumulation does?

43
00:01:51,739 --> 00:01:54,140
If mass production standardizes objects,

44
00:01:54,219 --> 00:01:57,242
can time and context reintroduce uniqueness?"

45
00:01:59,019 --> 00:02:02,420
Cent #8462, a great Cent.
